answer:
**Done.** I added one feature to [script.js](lab:open?file=script.js&line=25), after the shell:

```js
  // Engrave BOX into the outside of the floor: 8 mm cap height, 0.6 mm deep.
  .emboss(j.region.text('BOX', { size: mm(8) }), mm(0.6), {
    on: j.feat('body').startFace(), op: 'cut', id: 'label',
  })
```

`j.feat('body').startFace()` is the outside of the bottom (normal −Z, the face the box sits on), `op: 'cut'` sinks the letters into it, `size: mm(8)` is the cap height. No params, sketch or earlier feature changed; nothing else references the feature, so there is nothing downstream to re-check.

**What I checked.** `run()` is clean: ok, no errors or warnings, 3 features, 88 faces, volume $15344.265 \to 15277.391\ \text{mm}^3$, a difference of $66.87\ \text{mm}^3 = 0.6\ \text{mm} \times 111.5\ \text{mm}^2$ of letter stroke — i.e. the cut really is 0.6 mm deep. The pocket's side walls and its floor sit at $z = 0.3$ and $z = 0.6$ with each letter's faces named `label/side[text.1]#n` and `label/start` — so the engraving rises from the outside bottom face into the 2 mm floor and leaves 1.4 mm of material, without breaking into the cavity. Geometry and bbox are otherwise unchanged.

**Unfinished.** I could not show you the result: the workbench views are `axo/front/side/plan` only, and a text cut on the underside is invisible from all four (the plan render after the edit looks identical to before). I also could not confirm from a drawing that the letters read correctly when the box is turned over rather than mirrored — the sketch sits on the bottom face in its own frame, and only a view from below or a section would settle it. If you want it legible from below and it turns out reversed, the fix is to mirror the region, e.g. wrap the same `j.region.text(…)` with the region's mirror or emboss on a sketch that is flipped in X.

